13:14:17 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It proves the studio secured rights broad enough to cover **that DVD release**—and possibly many future uses—but not necessarily “for good” in every format, territory, or future platform. A few reasons your ’90s DVDs can still have the original songs: - **The license included home video.** By the 1990s, studios knew VHS/DVD were important, so music deals often covered them. - **The rights were perpetual.** Some licenses really are “in perpetuity,” especially for major films with larger budgets. - **The DVD was released while the license term was still active.** The rights might have been licensed for, say, 20 or 30 years. - **The particular song was inexpensive or easy to clear.** Not every song has a difficult licensing situation. - **The studio owns or controls the music rights.** For example, a studio affiliated with a record label or publisher may have had an easier deal. Also, a DVD already manufactured is not evidence about later versions. The studio may have had the right to make and sell that pressing, but a new remaster, a streaming upload, or a release in another country can be treated as a separate licensed exploitation. So: sometimes, yes, the studio did lock it in broadly enough that the song can stay forever. Your DVDs are consistent with that. The point is that it is **not automatic** just because the movie was once released with the song.
